----------------------------------------- Register form ---------------------------------------------

- BUILD A MORE COMPLEX REGISTRATION FORM WITH SIGNAL FORMS.

- Instructions: Angular Prerequisite "Signal Forms" chapter

1️⃣ Users must be able to register themselves into the application. We will thus add a register
    component, with a slightly more complex validation than the previous login form.
    let’s use signal forms for it.
    Create the register component with Angular CLI.


2️⃣ Add FormRoot and FormField (from @angular/forms/signals) to its imports.
    Then add a field named userForm to our new component, initialized using form(). The form model
    must contain 4 attributes:
    👉 a login control, initialized with an empty string, that is mandatory and should have at
        least 3 characters;
    👉 a required password field, initialized with an empty string;
    👉 a required confirmPassword field, initialized with an empty string;
    👉 a birthYear field, of type number | null, initialized with null, that is mandatory as well
        (you’ll need null as number | null to help TypeScript infer that null is in fact a
        number | null).
    

3️⃣ The template must of course display these four fields. The field login is of type text; the
    field password is of type password; the field confirmPassword is of type password; the field
    birthYear is of type number.
    The fields must of course be inside a form. Add the FormRoot directive to the form element with
    [formRoot]="userForm". The form declaration must use the submission.action option to call an
    async method register, to define in the component.

    👉 A div with the invalid-feedback class should display the message "Login is required" if the
    login is touched and has the required error. The field login should be required and have at
    least 3 characters. A div with the invalid-feedback class should display the message
    "Your login should be at least 3 characters" if the login isn’t long enough (minLength error)
    and if the user has started interacting with that field.

    👉 The password confirmation must have a validator checking that the two password values are
    equal. To achieve that, use validate() on the confirmPassword field. This validator must return
    an object with kind set to matchingError if the values don’t match, or undefined if they do
    match.

    👉Add a div with the invalid-feedback class, which must display "Your password does not match"
    if the user has started interacting with the confirmation field and if the values don’t match.
    The password field should also have a div with the invalid-feedback class displaying the
    message "Password is required" if the password is touched and has the required error. The
    confirmPassword field should have a div with the invalid-feedback class displaying the message
    "Password confirmation is required" if the password confirmation is touched and has the
    required error.

    👉 The last field to validate is birthYear. We could define a custom validator to check that
    the entered year is valid, but we can also do it easily with the min() and max() validators.
    We will consider a date valid if it’s at least equal to 1900, and at most the current year.
    The current year can be obtained using new Date().getFullYear().
    If the year is invalid, then display a div with the invalid-feedback class containing the
    message: "This is not a valid year". If the year is required, display a div with the
    invalid-feedback class containing the message: "Birth year is required".

    Add a button of type submit at the end of the form, containing the text Let's go!.


4️⃣ The remaining task is to send the registration information to the server. To do that, let’s
    add a new method in our UserService, called register(login, password, birthYear) which expects
    three parameters: the login, the password, and the birth year. Check the API documentation
    (https://ponyracer.ninja-squad.com/apidoc#resources-user-create) to know what it’s supposed to
    do.


5️⃣ Implement the async register method of the component, by delegating to the UserService. The
    signal form will call this method only if the form is valid, and will mark invalid fields as
    touched automatically.
    In this method, import firstValueFrom from rxjs and use it to await the service call. If the
    registration succeeds, redirect to the home page, using router.navigateByUrl(). Otherwise,
    set a new signal registrationFailed of the component to true.
    This signal allows displaying a div with the CSS classes alert alert-danger and the
    accessibility attribute role="alert" in the template, displaying the message "Try again with
    another login.".

    
6️⃣ Finally, Add a route configuration so that the URL /register goes to that new component.
    Add a link on the home page to navigate to that route (just after the Login link).


7️⃣ Do not forget to copy all the test files and run ng test to be sure that everything is OK. 